From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maago <maago@example.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 03:03:28 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Me

A signed-in user sees their own posts: /me in the browser, GET /api/me
for a frontend with an API token.

- The query my_posts.sql: the posts of author_id with their boards'
  names, newest first. Drafts and scheduled posts are in (they are
  mine); hidden posts are out
- One view, Me, serves two routes:

      maago.GET("/me", "me", Me),
      maago.GET("/api/me", "api_me", Me, maago.API),

      func Me(ctx context.Context, u auth.User, db maago.DB, in struct{}) (MePage, error)

  me is a page, so MePage draws with me_page.html; api_me has maago.API,
  so the same MePage goes out as JSON. u auth.User makes both need a
  sign-in: a browser goes to the sign-in page, an API call without a
  token gets a 401 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer
- What goes out is a MePage (the email and the posts), not the
  auth.User: the whole User in the JSON would send the password's hash
  too
- A link to my posts in base.html's header

To give User a field (a nickname, say), write it in User in
auth/models.go and run maago makemigrations: what maago add copied is
this project's code now. This tutorial leaves auth/ out of its patches,
so it does not.

The test sees ann's draft, published and scheduled posts, and not her
hidden one or bob's, on the page and in the JSON.

The table's rows for me and api_me: nobody is sent to sign in, or gets
a 401 from the API; everybody signed in gets their own.

